WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.880
Rotifers graze on algae in a chemostat.

2
00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:11.020
That is a vessel with a steady flow of nutrient medium in, and a matching flow out.

3
00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:14.240
Both populations rise and fall in cycles.

4
00:00:15.260 --> 00:00:16.420
Watch when the rotifers peak.

5
00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:19.760
They peak after the algae do, but how long after?

6
00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:23.880
That delay is a clue, and this film asks what it can tell us.

7
00:00:24.783 --> 00:00:29.763
In classical predator-prey models, the predator peak lags the prey peak by about a

8
00:00:29.763 --> 00:00:30.763
quarter of a cycle.

9
00:00:31.603 --> 00:00:35.243
Prey rise, predators feed and multiply, prey crash, predators starve.

10
00:00:36.163 --> 00:00:40.623
The phase lag is the delay between peaks, as a fraction of the cycle's period.

11
00:00:41.583 --> 00:00:45.283
Phase lag in cycles equals delay in days over period in days.

12
00:00:46.317 --> 00:00:48.157
Here is an invented example.

13
00:00:49.077 --> 00:00:50.457
Assume the cycles are regular.

14
00:00:51.497 --> 00:00:55.417
Prey peak on days 10, 40 and 70, so the period is 30 days.

15
00:00:56.337 --> 00:00:59.477
In run A, the rotifers peak on day 17.5.

16
00:01:00.037 --> 00:01:02.437
That is 7.5 days after the prey.

17
00:01:03.297 --> 00:01:06.757
7.5 over 30 is a quarter cycle, the classical lag.

18
00:01:07.750 --> 00:01:11.210
In run B, the rotifers peak on day 25 instead.

19
00:01:12.090 --> 00:01:13.770
That is 15 days after the prey.

20
00:01:14.610 --> 00:01:16.730
15 over 30 is one half cycle.

21
00:01:17.570 --> 00:01:20.110
The predator peaks now sit at the prey troughs.

22
00:01:20.510 --> 00:01:22.290
That pattern is called antiphase.

23
00:01:23.233 --> 00:01:27.513
Yoshida and colleagues grew the rotifer Brachionus on the alga Chlorella.

24
00:01:28.393 --> 00:01:30.513
The alga was either one clone or several.

25
00:01:31.373 --> 00:01:34.093
One clone cannot evolve by selection among clones.

26
00:01:35.213 --> 00:01:40.673
Several can. Single-clone prey gave short cycles with roughly the classical quarter

27
00:01:40.673 --> 00:01:45.713
lag. Multiclonal prey gave long cycles, with peaks largely out of step.

28
00:01:46.553 --> 00:01:49.713
In the real experiment the two treatments did not share a period.

29
00:01:49.713 --> 00:01:53.813
In our example, run B resembles the evolving-prey runs.

30
00:01:54.883 --> 00:01:57.903
But the lag alone does not prove that the algae evolved.

31
00:01:58.743 --> 00:02:00.383
A long lag could have other causes.

32
00:02:01.303 --> 00:02:05.383
The causal case came from changing the clonal diversity, and from a measured

33
00:02:05.383 --> 00:02:10.463
trade-off. Algae that resisted grazing were poorer competitors for nutrients.

34
00:02:11.303 --> 00:02:12.843
The study showed prey evolution.

35
00:02:13.723 --> 00:02:15.823
It did not investigate predator evolution.

36
00:02:16.683 --> 00:02:17.683
So keep this rule.

37
00:02:18.563 --> 00:02:19.903
A phase lag is a pattern.

38
00:02:20.663 --> 00:02:23.563
The causal case comes from changing the prey's variation.
